#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120688} :activity/id #uuid "01aaa671-aae7-587d-86f6-117d15d3fbee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049222 :topic/id #uuid "e4770b01-d5aa-5267-93ca-20f83709e3b1" :topic/math-academy-id 1855} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121040 :tutorial/content "A **basis** of a subspace $U$ (in $\\mathbb{R}^n$) is a set of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that is linearly independent and spans $U.$\n\nFor example, consider the following subspace consisting of the span of three vectors:\n![](images/aa/aaa76e82d8d54b305cfcfb0414f1fe7dd4d1c9d3ff932b4206a7b864922b394e.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U,$ we need to find a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $U.$\n\n**Watch out!** The set\n![](images/23/237305d1b82f44e0c365f721a33c35d8d983bd5facd4471d1885e8180ec7267e.svg)\nis *not* a basis of $U.$ It's true that this set *spans* $U,$ but the vectors in this set are *not* linearly independent. In particular, the vector $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d0ff277adbace09315a1c0cd7c964eac41f5e2f080d0e32f888f379be435da0.svg)\n\nTo find a basis of $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ we can start with $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ but we need to remove any \"redundant\" vectors that can be written as a linear combination of other vectors in the set. In this case, we remove ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}$ because it can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nWe are left with the set\n\n![](images/79/79c529aa2fb5373ff9f8798300b1423ea63f90778bd4832ba1276f6af9c21f04.svg)\n\nThese two vectors are linearly independent (since they are not parallel), and removing a redundant vector does not change the span, so these vectors still span $U.$ That is to say,\n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\} = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ is a basis of $U.$\n\n**Note:** To see that the vectors $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ still span $U,$ first note that any vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in U$ can be written as a linear combination of the three original vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1} + x_2 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} + x_3 {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} = -{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}+3{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ then we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){a}_{1}+({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){a}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{b}$ can actually be written as a linear combination of only two vectors, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the set $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ spans the subspace $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ff21f5d-5007-5b74-b135-bdb080fd5541" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "be88e95e-9faa-5bfe-b721-4eda45231dc0" :step/math-academy-id 17504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119626 :question/id #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4816"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429769}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "1169ef7f-a019-5cfa-8ef6-1d2ff514f2f1" :step/math-academy-id 17505 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121064 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, it was easy to see that one vector was a linear combination of the others. But what can we do when it's not so easy to tell? For example, consider the subspace\n\n![](images/4c/4c12a6e56a6cc7cd72371c59f41b33d8876cf4b98aa40766f0e67ad1bc3a32f7.svg)\n\nTo find a subset of linearly independent vectors, we can put the vectors in a matrix and compute the reduced row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent (or redundant) vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\n**But watch out!** We need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $U$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/1d/1dd0ca9bae9a29dea8fc34ed3d9fc453a1d52c48f1f19a95c2fb16c65cd9c7d8.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c0f5ce4-8098-51b1-a25e-8cb4d84505c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3945 :tutorial/title "Finding a Basis by Identifying Pivot Columns"} :step/id #uuid "dee9e822-2a94-59f6-9320-08d94fcf8912" :step/math-academy-id 23378 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119672 :question/id #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6237"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427561}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"} :step/id #uuid "19b9d2e4-cc12-5198-97ee-5424e005fc32" :step/math-academy-id 23379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121065 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we computed a basis of \n\n![](images/94/944c607bf892f0d346df79dfa08e6bfec699e64feac018d40a733be0a5c11041.svg)\n\nThe basis that we found was\n\n![](images/70/70a26164bd64f6e4483b38a08187cac2e1498df7f9066e2be9a295e7b04459da.svg)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n![](images/2a/2a92f6d5b67012d342685436d6ccc0ae1670791a060129b10c7d858cafda7b26.svg)\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n![](images/4d/4defcc004e360ec2e87798b158616575f284ae36369c766ee950c20ae06eff0a.svg)\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n![](images/1b/1be8cce3195aa633e76e855eba391dd8d1342cf0f3a19e0302eaf67e2828b909.svg)\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=4$ and $x_2=5,$ meaning that\n![](images/cf/cf5185386d46084579b56fc6ad00769c7c5e7a0d2f320be7ccb28aa4961519ae.svg)\n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "14b13853-b5c6-5bc9-825c-a11cd19dff93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3968 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "3fd42240-5623-532c-921e-a37910ca748c" :step/math-academy-id 23546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119673 :question/id #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" :question/math-academy-id "e-6257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427560}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"} :step/id #uuid "e1cd2039-adf1-5e16-9c01-414506e00c8e" :step/math-academy-id 23547}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120049}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427562} :activity/id #uuid "81a180d3-6c3b-57f0-9044-6bd27b374a8d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049222 :topic/id #uuid "e4770b01-d5aa-5267-93ca-20f83709e3b1" :topic/math-academy-id 1855} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5646c621-9ff4-5c1e-8500-97102b3d30e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427545} {:answer/id #uuid "830afe53-3ec3-5e7b-9c3c-099ac06955ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427546} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac2a6dd-782d-5d5d-9fa2-a488442ba8e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427547} {:answer/id #uuid "deb5edd7-a250-52b2-967e-5c46f246d2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427553} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e5049e-edad-5162-bbb3-262144109a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427555}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427553} :answer-field/id #uuid "52b0b14b-3418-5c43-b479-6c5ffa179aac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dd91b565-0528-53ef-9896-e748c3fd0d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75068" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 16 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19a71a95-255d-5e73-bf84-db0c055cc314" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427559 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b48259-01c4-5109-abc6-4dc67b3a3146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427541} {:answer/id #uuid "b70926c5-b359-5ad0-bd7d-b705946cb78b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427548} {:answer/id #uuid "bd4b0fe3-0884-5aa4-93af-8002f7bc5198" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427549} {:answer/id #uuid "d713060d-1719-5dcc-a1f8-8b656c01f943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427552} {:answer/id #uuid "f441b186-2f4b-5de4-917f-48c65dfbcc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427549} :answer-field/id #uuid "a651d1bb-f837-5587-b77d-257e4a384de7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0010b93b-f332-5a69-9522-ddec46829e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-110852" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ ${a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-16 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}-9 & 8 & -16 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1ce228df-2e95-52de-a0e7-a808a0d5cd03" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f11080-8e79-55d1-814d-39c81b12ba97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427542} {:answer/id #uuid "15dddeb9-e2d3-55d4-9c4e-ed6b802b40f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427543} {:answer/id #uuid "399d1ce8-2eb3-525d-b9b3-45ffc5c60921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427544} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bc1d3c-02a8-5212-85ed-899170ed9e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427550} {:answer/id #uuid "cca65a09-cc23-5ec7-990f-956f30407ce7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427550} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01e595b-9131-553f-970b-e23a7aab76e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7ffe635-df11-5df3-b5e3-505ac6562519" :question/math-academy-id "q-110895" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B=\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=5 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 7 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 1=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "1f299c67-ea42-572c-80e2-7c614418b73d"}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427540}]}
